{"name":"Blue Fjord","hex":"#007290","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"fjord","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A long, narrow, deep inlet between cliffs."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fjord"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fjord","blocks":[{"text":"In physical geography, a fjord is a long, relatively narrow sea inlet with steep sides or cliffs in a valley created by a former glacier, which has since become inundated with water. They are the glacial equivalent of drowned river valleys, known as rias. Fjords exist on the coasts of Antarctica, the Arctic, and surrounding landmasses of the northern and southern hemispheres. Areas with extensive fjords demonstrate an extreme example of the coastline paradox: Norway's coastline is estimated to be 29,000 km (18,000 mi) long with its nearly 1,200 fjords, but only 2,500 km (1,600 mi) long when excluding the fjords."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Geirangerfjord_%286-2007%29.jpg/330px-Geirangerfjord_%286-2007%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fjord"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Glacier_in_eastern_Greenland.jpg/500px-Glacier_in_eastern_Greenland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Glacier_in_eastern_Greenland.jpg/1280px-Glacier_in_eastern_Greenland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Glacier_in_eastern_Greenland.jpg/500px-Glacier_in_eastern_Greenland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A glacier in eastern Greenland flowing through a fjord carved by the movement of ice","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGlacier_in_eastern_Greenland.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Fjord_genesis.png/500px-Fjord_genesis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Fjord_genesis.png/500px-Fjord_genesis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Illustration of how a fjord is created","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFjord_genesis.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Odda_fr%C3%A5_fly.jpg/500px-Odda_fr%C3%A5_fly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Odda_fr%C3%A5_fly.jpg/500px-Odda_fr%C3%A5_fly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fjords often continue as U-shaped valleys above sea level and freshwater fjord lakes: Sørfjorden (Hardanger) can be seen here transitioning into Sandvinvatnet and the Odda Valley. Odda sits on the isthmus, Folgefonna on the right.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOdda_fr%C3%A5_fly.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Muldalsfossen.JPG/500px-Muldalsfossen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Muldalsfossen.JPG/1280px-Muldalsfossen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Muldalsfossen.JPG/500px-Muldalsfossen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Muldalsfossen waterfall drops several hundred meters from the Muldalen hanging valley to Tafjorden.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMuldalsfossen.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Weichsel-W%C3%BCrm-Glaciation.png/500px-Weichsel-W%C3%BCrm-Glaciation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Weichsel-W%C3%BCrm-Glaciation.png/1280px-Weichsel-W%C3%BCrm-Glaciation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Weichsel-W%C3%BCrm-Glaciation.png/500px-Weichsel-W%C3%BCrm-Glaciation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Distribution of ice (white) in Europe during the last glacial period","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWeichsel-W%C3%BCrm-Glaciation.png"},{"srcset":"htt